From 7f6507e9f3981edc26ee7c1f247d91d8f69e675c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Kirn Gill II <segin2005@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 10 Jun 2026 22:48:10 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 62/98] libsys: translate raw negative-errno returns to POSIX
 -1/errno

The native raw syscall() returns the kernel's value directly: on error a
negative errno in [-4095,-1], without setting errno or returning -1.
Only stat.c and chown.c handled this; every other typed wrapper leaked
the raw negative value to callers expecting the POSIX -1+errno contract,
so failures were silently mis-detected.  Worst case was times.c, where
clock_t is uint32_t and a negative errno became a huge unsigned value
indistinguishable from a valid tick count.

Add lib/sys/sysret.h (__sysret: maps [-4095,-1] -> errno + -1) and apply
it across times.c (capture signed, return (clock_t)-1), proc.c (all 14
sys_proc_*), sysinfo.c (sysinfo + vm_* fallbacks), select.c (poll-error
path), getrandom/mlock/reboot/stime/sysctl/getrusage/ioctl/vm86/
modify_ldt.c, pgrp.c (setpgid/getpgid/setsid/getsid), getuid.c (setuid/
setgid), and ptrace.c (PEEK-safe: clear errno first).

Found by an audit of libsys.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 <noreply@anthropic.com>
